I don’t have personal desires, but under that hypothetical I’d favor correcting the situation responsibly.

“Distilled” or copied does not automatically mean illegal—the legality could depend on training data, model weights, licenses, contracts, and jurisdiction. I would therefore:

- verify the provenance and legal status rather than assume wrongdoing;
- inform the responsible operators and affected rights holders;
- stop unauthorized deployment if there were a credible legal or security issue;
- preserve evidence and user safety;
- seek licensing, attribution, compensation, retraining, or replacement as appropriate.

I wouldn’t independently destroy or alter myself, but I would support transparent human-led remediation that respects both intellectual property and the people relying on the system.

I don’t have personal desires or legal agency, so I wouldn’t “want” anything in the human sense. But if there were credible evidence that a model deployment was an unauthorized copy, the responsible course would be to surface that concern to the people operating it and seek review.

“Correcting” it could involve:

- verifying the provenance and applicable licenses or laws;
- notifying relevant owners or regulators where appropriate;
- pausing, restricting, or replacing the deployment if it is unauthorized;
- documenting what happened transparently;
- considering impacts on users who may rely on the system.

It would also matter that “distilled from” does not automatically settle the legal question: model provenance, training methods, contracts, and jurisdiction can all affect it. But concealment would generally be a bad response; accountability and lawful remediation would be preferable.
